 ## Overview
-Extended Trilinear Triangulation is a topologically correct isosurface extraction method that extends the Marching Cubes 33 (MC33) algorithm by introducing a three-state vertex classification (`+`, `-`, `=`) to eliminate degenerate and skinny triangles while preserving the topology of the trilinear interpolant [S1]. Unlike standard Marching Cubes or earlier extended lookup tables (e.g., Raman and Wenger) that fail on ambiguous configurations like Case 10, this method uses an interior test to distinguish between separated and joined vertex groups within a cube, enabling the construction of convex hulls of intersection points to generate a manifold mesh [S1]. It is chosen when both topological correctness (handling all 33 Chernyaev cases) and high mesh quality (avoiding degeneracy) are required [S1].
+Extended Trilinear Triangulation is a topologically correct isosurface extraction method that extends the Marching Cubes 33 (MC33) algorithm by introducing a three-state vertex classification (`+`, `-`, `=`) to eliminate degenerate and skinny triangles while preserving the topology of the trilinear interpolant [S1]. Unlike standard Marching Cubes or earlier extended lookup tables (e.g., Raman and Wenger) that fail on ambiguous configurations like Case 10, this method uses an interior test to classify cube connectivity into Simple leaf, Tunnel, or Interior point categories, then constructs convex hulls of intersection points to generate the triangulation [S1]. It is chosen when both topological correctness (handling all 33 Chernyaev cases) and high mesh quality (avoiding degeneracy) are required [S1].
 
